\chapter{Tutorial: Letting an agent use canvaslms}
\label{tutorial-agent-skills}

[[agent-skills]] is for a user who has finished the earlier tutorials
and wants a coding agent, Claude Code in particular, to run
[[canvaslms]] on their behalf: to answer \enquote{what is due next
week in my course?} by running the commands rather than by guessing.
The object of learning is not [[canvaslms]] itself but how an agent
comes to use it well: through a \emph{skill}, a Markdown file that
tells the agent when the tool applies and how to use it, and through
the boundaries the user sets around what the agent may do.

The skills for [[canvaslms]] live in the author's public skills
repository.\footnote{\url{https://github.com/dbosk/claude-skills}}
Three of them matter here:
\begin{description}
\item[canvas-info] teaches a read-only research workflow: find the
  course, list what it contains, view the items, refine with regexes.
\item[canvas-quiz] teaches how to write quiz files that
  [[canvaslms quizzes]] uploads, so it writes to Canvas.
\item[kth-data] names the author's sandbox course for testing writing
  commands, and other facts an agent cannot know.
\end{description}

The critical aspects are these:
\begin{description}
\item[A skill is a file] The agent knows nothing about [[canvaslms]]
  until it reads one; the learner reads the file first, so that what
  the agent does later is not magic.
\item[Read-only versus writing] The skills split along the same line
  the grading tutorial drew, and an agent should be given the reading
  skill by default and the writing ones deliberately.
\item[The agent runs the same commands] A non-interactive agent call
  is one more shell command, and the tool permission it is given
  names [[canvaslms]] and nothing else.
\end{description}

The arc is:
\begin{enumerate}
\item obtain the skills and read the research skill (steps 1--3);
\item match tasks to skills (steps 4--5);
\item install a skill for one's own agent (step~6);
\item find out whether the agent is installed and compose a
  non-interactive call (steps 7--8);
\item name what the agent must not do unsupervised (step~9).
\end{enumerate}

\section{What this tutorial does not run}

Two commands are composed as answers rather than run.
Installing a skill copies files into the learner's own agent
configuration, and the tutorial cannot know what is already there; a
tutorial that ran [[cp]] into [[~/.claude/skills]] could overwrite a
checkout the learner maintains.
Step~1 therefore clones the repository into the tutorial's scratch
workspace, and step~6 asks for the install command as text.

Calling the agent is composed as text for more reasons.
The [[claude]] command may not be installed, it needs an account and
the network, it costs money and takes time, and its output is not the
same twice, so there is nothing a shell step could validate.
A shell step would pass on the typed line alone, including for a
learner who just saw \emph{command not found}.
And a learner who runs this tutorial \emph{inside} a Claude Code
session would start an agent inside the agent.
Step~7 establishes honestly whether the command exists and step~8
teaches the shape of the call; the instructions invite the learner to
run it for real afterwards.

Steps 2 and 3 match text in the skills repository, which is not part
of [[canvaslms]].
They match only two strings that a rewrite of the skill is unlikely to
lose, the first words of its description and the phrase
\emph{read-only}, and in both steps the typed command is the pattern
that carries the step.

\section{Tutorial source}

<<[[agent-skills.md]]>>=
---
id: agent-skills
title: Letting an agent use canvaslms
summary: Read the canvaslms skills, match tasks to skills, install one, and compose a safe agent call.
---

<<get the skills step>>

<<read the skill step>>

<<find the boundary step>>

<<pick the research skill step>>

<<pick the writing skills step>>

<<install a skill step>>

<<check for the agent step>>

<<compose an agent call step>>

<<name the forbidden commands step>>
@

\section{Step 1: Get the skills}

The skills are a Git repository, and cloning it into the workspace
gives the learner the files without touching their own configuration.
The pattern also accepts [[ls]] on an existing [[~/.claude/skills]],
for a learner who has the repository already or is offline; the later
steps only need the [[canvas-info/SKILL.md]] path to exist somewhere
they can read it.

<<get the skills step>>=
# Get the skills

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:git\s+clone[^\n]*claude-skills|(?:^|\s)ls\s[^\n]*\.claude/skills)
hint: "Clone the claude-skills repository from GitHub into a directory named skills here in the workspace. If you already have it under ~/.claude/skills, list that directory instead."
```

Run `git clone https://github.com/dbosk/claude-skills.git skills`.

This is the author's collection of skills for Claude Code. A skill is
a directory with a `SKILL.md` file, and an agent that has the
directory in its skills folder reads the file when the task matches
its description. Cloning into this workspace keeps the tutorial away
from your own configuration; step 6 is where you install.

If you already have the repository as `~/.claude/skills`, run
`ls ~/.claude/skills` instead and use that path in the next steps.

Exit the shell when the clone is done.
@

\section{Step 2: Read what the skill tells the agent}

Before any agent runs anything, the learner reads what it will read.
The first sixty lines of [[canvas-info/SKILL.md]] hold the front
matter, whose [[description]] is what makes the agent pick the skill,
and the start of the workflow.
The match on the first words of the description ties the step to the
content, not just to the command.

<<read the skill step>>=
# Read what the skill tells the agent

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)(?:cat|less|more|head|sed)\b[^\n]*canvas-info/SKILL\.md
  - Research and answer questions about Canvas LMS courses
hint: "Print the first part of skills/canvas-info/SKILL.md with head or sed. The description in the front matter is what the agent matches a task against."
```

Run `head -n 60 skills/canvas-info/SKILL.md`.

The file starts with front matter between two `---` lines. Its
`description` is what the agent reads to decide whether the skill
applies to a task: it names the situations, such as a question about
deadlines or a mention of a course code. The body that follows is the
workflow the agent then follows, and it is the same sequence you used
in the earlier tutorials: `courses` to find the course, `list` verbs
to see what it contains, `view` verbs to read items.

Exit the shell when you have read the front matter and the start of
the workflow.
@

\section{Step 3: Find the read-only boundary}

The skill says of itself that it is read-only, and [[grep]] finds
where.
The point of the step is that the boundary is written into the skill,
not enforced by the tool: an agent with this skill will not grade, but
only because the file tells it not to and points at another skill for
writing.
That is what the learner needs to know before choosing which skills
to give an agent.

<<find the boundary step>>=
# Find the read-only boundary

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)grep\b[^\n]*canvas-info/SKILL\.md
  - read-only
hint: "grep for read-only in the same file."
```

Run `grep -n read-only skills/canvas-info/SKILL.md`.

The skill states that it only retrieves information and that writing
quiz content belongs to another skill, `canvas-quiz`. Nothing in
`canvaslms` enforces that: the agent has the same `submissions grade`
you met in the grading tutorial. The boundary holds because the skill
draws it and because you choose which skills the agent has.

Exit the shell when you have found the line.
@

\section{Step 4: Which skill for this job?}

A single-select question with a reading task.
The three options are the three skills named in the introduction, so
the learner has to match the task to a description rather than
recognise a name.

<<pick the research skill step>>=
# Which skill for this job?

```tutorial-step
kind: single_select
options:
  - canvas-info
  - canvas-quiz
  - kth-data
answers:
  - canvas-info
hint: "The task only reads: which skill describes a research workflow?"
```

You ask the agent: *summarise what is due next week in my course*.
Which skill should it use?
@

\section{Step 5: And for this one?}

The counterpart: a writing task that also needs a place to try the
writing safely.
Two skills apply, which is why this is a multi-select, and the second
of them, [[kth-data]], is the one that names a sandbox course.
The option [[canvas-info]] is wrong here, since writing a quiz is
outside what it allows.

<<pick the writing skills step>>=
# And for this one?

```tutorial-step
kind: multi_select
options:
  - canvas-info
  - canvas-quiz
  - kth-data
answers:
  - canvas-quiz
  - kth-data
hint: "One skill writes quizzes; another knows where writing can be tried without harm."
```

You ask the agent: *write a quiz on this week's lecture and try
uploading it somewhere it cannot do harm*. Which skills does it need?

The writing skill is the obvious one. The other names a sandbox
course, a course that exists only to be broken, and the rule that
writing commands are tried there first with anchored regexes. If you
give an agent a writing skill, give it a sandbox too, or a skill of
your own that names one.
@

\section{Step 6: Install them for your own agent}

The install is composed rather than run (see above), and the answer
regex accepts any of the usual ways to put a directory under
[[~/.claude/skills]]: a copy, a link, [[rsync]], or a clone straight
into place.

<<install a skill step>>=
# Install them for your own agent

```tutorial-step
kind: input
answers:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: ^(?:cp|ln|rsync|install|git)\b[^\n]*\.claude/skills
hint: "One command that puts the canvas-info directory under ~/.claude/skills: cp -r, ln -s, rsync or a git clone into place."
```

Type, without running it, the command that would install the
`canvas-info` skill for your own Claude Code, for example
`cp -r skills/canvas-info ~/.claude/skills/`.

Claude Code reads skills from `~/.claude/skills`, one directory per
skill. Copying gives you a snapshot; a symbolic link or a clone of the
whole repository into that path keeps you on the author's updates. Run
the command after this tutorial if you want the skill; the tutorial
does not touch your configuration.
@

\section{Step 7: Is the agent installed?}

An honest check: [[command -v]] prints the path or nothing, and the
step passes either way, since the tutorial cannot require an agent to
be installed.
What the learner learns is whether step~8 can be run for real.

<<check for the agent step>>=
# Is the agent installed?

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)(?:command\s+-v|which|type)\s+claude\b
hint: "Ask the shell whether claude is on the PATH with command -v claude."
```

Run `command -v claude || echo "claude is not installed"`.

If a path is printed, Claude Code is installed and you can run the
call you compose in the next step. If not, the next step still teaches
the shape of the call; install Claude Code afterwards if you want to
use it.

Exit the shell when you know the answer.
@

\section{Step 8: Compose a non-interactive run}

The call is composed as text, with the two flags that make an agent
call scriptable and safe: [[-p]] runs one prompt and exits, and
[[--allowedTools]] names exactly the commands the agent may run without
asking, here [[canvaslms]] and nothing else.
The answer regex requires [[claude]] and [[-p]] or [[--print]]; the
prompt and the tool list are the learner's.

<<compose an agent call step>>=
# Compose a non-interactive run

```tutorial-step
kind: input
answers:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: ^(\S+\s+)?claude\b(?=[^\n]*\s(?:-p|--print)\b)
hint: "claude -p with a question in quotes, and --allowedTools to permit canvaslms and nothing else."
```

Type the command that asks Claude Code one question about your course
and lets it run `canvaslms` to answer, for example
`claude -p "Which assignments in DD1301 are ungraded?" --allowedTools "Bash(canvaslms:*)"`.

`-p` prints one answer and exits instead of opening a session, which
is how you use the agent from a script. `--allowedTools` lists the
tools it may use without asking; `Bash(canvaslms:*)` permits shell
commands that start with `canvaslms` and nothing else, so the agent
can read your course but cannot, say, delete files. With the
`canvas-info` skill installed, the agent runs the same `courses` and
`list` commands you ran in the earlier tutorials. If step 7 found the
command, run this for real once you have installed the skill.
@

\section{Step 9: What must the agent never do unsupervised?}

The closing question returns to the boundary from the grading
tutorial, now applied to an agent.
The options mix three reading commands with the two writing ones the
tutorials have named, and the answer is the same as it was for the
learner: the writing verbs are run only after their reading
counterparts have shown what they match, and by an agent only when a
person is watching.

<<name the forbidden commands step>>=
# What must the agent never do unsupervised?

```tutorial-step
kind: multi_select
options:
  - canvaslms courses
  - canvaslms submissions grade
  - canvaslms pages edit
  - canvaslms results
  - canvaslms assignments list
answers:
  - canvaslms submissions grade
  - canvaslms pages edit
hint: "The same two kinds of verbs as in the grading tutorial: which of these change something in Canvas?"
```

Which of these should an agent never run without a person watching?

The reading commands are safe to allow: the worst an agent can do
with them is read the wrong course. The writing commands change
grades and pages, and the agent has no way to know that a regex
matched more than you meant. Keep those to interactive sessions where
you approve each command, or to a sandbox course, and give the agent
the read-only skill by default.
@
